WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.300
So what if I told you that 2 plus 2 doesn't actually equal 4?

00:06.100 --> 00:08.420
Well, I mean, you probably get some very confused looks.

00:08.620 --> 00:09.400
Right, exactly.

00:09.640 --> 00:16.440
But what if saying 2 plus 2 equals 4 is, like, completely ignoring the most fundamental part of mathematics,

00:16.780 --> 00:18.900
which is, you know, how you actually got there?

00:19.180 --> 00:20.820
Oh, I see where you're going with this.

00:20.900 --> 00:24.020
Yeah, because we're basically taught to treat math like an x-ray.

00:24.500 --> 00:28.940
You know, you look at the image, you see the broken bone, and the doctor just points and says, there it is.

00:28.940 --> 00:32.500
Right, it's treated as this collection of cold, hard facts.

00:32.600 --> 00:32.940
Exactly.

00:33.360 --> 00:36.220
Objects that just, like, exist in the universe.

00:36.520 --> 00:39.120
We think of them as just waiting for us to point at them.

00:39.220 --> 00:40.140
2 plus 2 is 4.

00:40.520 --> 00:41.920
A triangle has three sides.

00:42.380 --> 00:46.120
It's clean, it's binary, and honestly, it's really comforting.

00:46.540 --> 00:47.020
It is.

00:47.140 --> 00:49.740
I mean, it gives us this sense of static permanence.

00:49.880 --> 00:53.960
Like, mathematical truths are just these axiomatic laws floating in the void, you know,

00:54.300 --> 00:56.760
completely independent of anything humans actually do.

00:56.760 --> 01:01.440
But for this deep dive, we are going to, like, completely tear that x-ray down.

01:02.660 --> 01:09.860
Because the stack of sources we're looking at today suggests that math isn't a collection of static objects at all.

01:09.960 --> 01:11.860
No, it's something entirely different.

01:11.960 --> 01:12.100
Yeah.

01:12.320 --> 01:15.580
What if math is actually a recorded history of choices?

01:16.360 --> 01:24.320
And what if the exact order in which things happen is fundamentally more important than whatever final answer you get at the end?

01:24.320 --> 01:26.560
I mean, that is just a philosophical earthquake.

01:26.700 --> 01:32.680
It's a total inversion of how we think about knowledge and logic and, well, reality itself.

01:32.820 --> 01:33.400
It really is.

01:33.640 --> 01:38.160
And our sources today are dense, they're weird, and they're just absolutely fascinating.

01:38.600 --> 01:45.340
So first we have these visual guides for what, at first glance, looks like a super simple mobile puzzle game called Sphere Pop.

01:45.540 --> 01:47.260
Right, with the little color bubbles.

01:47.260 --> 01:47.960
Yeah, exactly.

01:48.300 --> 01:54.160
But alongside that game, we have this extensive suite of documents detailing an entire educational framework.

01:54.360 --> 01:55.980
It's called the Sproul Curriculum.

01:56.120 --> 01:57.080
Which is quite the rabbit hole.

01:57.400 --> 01:57.880
Oh, completely.

01:58.100 --> 02:01.580
It includes actual children booklets, specifically the very first one.

02:01.680 --> 02:04.760
So Sproul 0001 and 02.

02:05.640 --> 02:12.240
And binding all of this together is a massive 50-plus page academic monograph.

02:12.500 --> 02:14.400
By an independent researcher named Flyxion, right?

02:14.400 --> 02:16.240
Yep, published in August 2026.

02:16.400 --> 02:23.420
And this monograph explains the deeply complex, like, architectural theory that's operating underneath these colorful little children's books.

02:23.480 --> 02:26.020
And we should clarify, this is definitely not a standard teacher syllabus.

02:26.460 --> 02:31.700
Flyxion as Monograph outlines how this curriculum uses a formal system, they call it the Sphere Pop Calculus,

02:32.180 --> 02:36.860
to basically shift the entire focus of learning from what is to what happened.

02:36.860 --> 02:37.880
Which is wild.

02:38.020 --> 02:47.140
So our mission for this deep dive is to explore how a simple game of popping digital bubbles can, like, scale up into this revolutionary philosophy of epistemology.

02:47.260 --> 02:50.640
Which is really just a fancy academic word for, you know, how we know what we know.

02:51.100 --> 02:51.580
Exactly.

02:51.580 --> 02:58.240
So whether you're listening because you want, like, a totally new framework for problem solving at your job,

02:58.420 --> 03:03.580
or you're just insanely curious about how we construct reality from scratch, stick around.

03:03.980 --> 03:07.320
You're going to walk away with some really profound tools for thinking today.

03:07.540 --> 03:08.000
Absolutely.

03:08.580 --> 03:10.140
So, okay, let's unpack this.

03:10.400 --> 03:12.640
I want to start by just looking at this game, Sphere Pop.

03:12.640 --> 03:19.960
Because when I first saw the visual guide, I honestly thought someone had sent us a manual for, like, a Candy Crush ripoff.

03:20.040 --> 03:20.740
Oh, for sure.

03:20.840 --> 03:22.660
The aesthetic is very deceptive.

03:22.740 --> 03:32.860
I mean, it uses these bright primary colors, rounded shapes, and, you know, the mechanics seem designed for this purely tactile, dopamine-driven experience.

03:33.100 --> 03:33.940
Yeah, it really does.

03:34.020 --> 03:36.740
And the instructions are based on super simple arithmetic.

03:37.180 --> 03:40.920
You start with an expression on your screen made of numbers, mathematical operations, and parentheses.

03:41.280 --> 03:41.640
Right.

03:41.640 --> 03:43.200
So the guide gives a basic example.

03:43.380 --> 03:50.640
It's open parenthesis, 4 plus 1, close parenthesis, and then right next to it, open parenthesis, 3 minus 1, close parenthesis.

03:51.260 --> 03:55.340
So mathematically, you're just looking at 4 plus 1 multiplied by 3 minus 1.

03:55.440 --> 03:55.780
Exactly.

03:56.220 --> 04:01.880
And the single governing rule of the game is that you can only interact with things that are enclosed in parentheses.

04:02.320 --> 04:03.880
Which is a clever constraint.

04:03.880 --> 04:14.200
Yeah, so on your turn, you tap a pop, a set of parentheses, by solving whatever localized equation is inside it.

04:14.580 --> 04:18.440
You literally just replace the bubble with the result of that specific calculation.

04:18.440 --> 04:20.220
So if you tap the 4 plus 1.

04:20.520 --> 04:20.740
Right.

04:20.800 --> 04:25.340
If I tap the first set, the 4 plus 1, it collapses into a 5, so now my whole board changes.

04:25.440 --> 04:28.060
Now it reads 5, and then in parentheses, 3 minus 1.

04:28.140 --> 04:28.340
Okay.

04:28.500 --> 04:28.920
Makes sense.

04:28.920 --> 04:31.000
And then on my next turn, I pop the second set.

04:31.520 --> 04:33.460
The 3 minus 1 collapses into a 2.

04:33.700 --> 04:35.800
So now my board is just 5, parenthesis 2.

04:36.120 --> 04:42.180
And then I make one final pop, multiply them together, and I'm left with a single sphere holding the number 10.

04:42.180 --> 04:45.900
And the game is just over when there are no parentheses left to pop, right?

04:45.900 --> 04:46.460
I got it.

04:46.880 --> 04:49.380
And the guide outlines, like, a few ways to play.

04:49.640 --> 04:55.040
You can compete for the highest final value, or race against the clock to collapse this massive equation.

04:55.240 --> 05:01.980
Or you can play dynamically, where you're trying to strategically choose which parentheses to pop, so you hit a specific target number.

05:02.240 --> 05:03.800
You know, it sounds really satisfying.

05:04.220 --> 05:04.660
It is.

05:04.820 --> 05:07.360
To me, playing it feels like digital bubble wrap.

05:07.360 --> 05:14.580
You take this mussy, kind of anxiety-inducing string of data, and you just methodically reduce it.

05:15.240 --> 05:15.820
Pop, pop, pop.

05:15.860 --> 05:17.260
Just narrowing down the noise.

05:17.380 --> 05:17.540
Yeah.

05:17.720 --> 05:26.040
It feels like an incredibly clever way to trick, like, a third grader into practicing their order of operations without them ever realizing they're doing math homework.

05:26.380 --> 05:26.940
Which it is.

05:26.980 --> 05:28.760
It's an elegant way to teach arithmetic.

05:29.280 --> 05:35.740
But looking at it as just a math game completely misses the actual architecture running underneath it.

05:35.760 --> 05:36.300
Oh, totally.

05:36.300 --> 05:44.120
Yeah, to see what the player's really doing, we have to transition to the second document in our sources, which is the Boolean synthesis schematic.

05:44.460 --> 05:50.020
Right, which looks like someone took a literal motherboard blueprint and, like, drew it with crayons.

05:50.120 --> 05:51.480
That's a perfect description, yeah.

05:51.680 --> 05:53.780
Because the game is actually a Trojan horse.

05:54.120 --> 05:55.740
It isn't just about arithmetic at all.

05:55.820 --> 05:58.880
It's a foundational engine for teaching logic gates.

05:58.980 --> 05:59.420
Wait, really?

05:59.860 --> 06:00.840
Like computer logic?

06:00.840 --> 06:01.440
Exactly.

06:01.960 --> 06:08.060
The mechanics of tapping those colorful bubbles are built on four highly specific mathematical primitives.

06:08.920 --> 06:10.880
Okay, I'm looking at this schematic right now.

06:11.060 --> 06:11.360
Uh-uh.

06:11.580 --> 06:16.620
Walk me through these four primitives, because this is where the math started to feel like a completely different language to me.

06:16.740 --> 06:17.000
Sure.

06:17.000 --> 06:27.220
So the first primitive is POP, and it's denoted formally by the letter P. The definition they use here is, quote, expose a value without alteration.

06:27.620 --> 06:27.860
Okay.

06:27.860 --> 06:35.300
So in a digital logic sense, it basically takes an incoming signal, let's call it X, and simply outputs X.

06:35.480 --> 06:38.680
So it just passes the information along, no changes at all.

06:38.760 --> 06:39.120
Exactly.

06:39.520 --> 06:47.720
Then the second primitive is refuse, denoted by R, and this operation inverts a value, so it takes that signal X and outputs not X.

06:47.900 --> 06:48.300
Got it.

06:48.320 --> 06:50.700
So if the input is a 1, it spits out a 0.

06:51.320 --> 06:54.040
If a statement is true, refuse turns it false.

06:54.160 --> 06:54.320
Right.

06:54.380 --> 06:55.400
It's a direct inversion.

06:55.400 --> 07:01.440
The third is by and D, denoted by B. Now, this operation requires two values to exist simultaneously.

07:01.600 --> 07:05.140
It takes an X and a Y and outputs a coupled state, so X, A, and D, Y.

07:05.220 --> 07:07.880
Okay, so they're yoked together. You basically can't have one without the other.

07:08.360 --> 07:08.760
Precisely.

07:09.180 --> 07:12.580
And the final primitive is collapse E, denoted by C.

07:13.420 --> 07:18.260
This commits the Boolean result, permanently locking the state into a 0 or a 1.

07:18.380 --> 07:20.080
It effectively closes the action.

07:20.240 --> 07:21.900
Okay, so let me make sure I have this.

07:21.900 --> 07:26.340
We have P, R, B, and C. Pop, refuse, bind, collapse.

07:26.660 --> 07:27.080
That's it.

07:27.220 --> 07:30.800
Okay, I'm following the definitions, but I have to, you know, push back here a little bit.

07:30.880 --> 07:31.060
Yeah.

07:31.220 --> 07:38.140
You're telling me these four highly abstract moves are secretly running inside a game where I'm just adding four in one.

07:38.500 --> 07:42.060
Like, why do these specific four operations matter so much?

07:42.060 --> 07:45.600
Because if you have those four operations, you don't just have a math game.

07:45.700 --> 07:48.260
You literally have the ability to construct a computer.

07:48.740 --> 07:48.840
Wow.

07:49.100 --> 07:54.900
Yeah, the synthesis document demonstrates how these specific primitives can build standard computing logic gates.

07:55.200 --> 07:59.420
For example, if you want to build a buffer gate, you just string together a pop and a collapse.

07:59.600 --> 07:59.840
Oh.

07:59.960 --> 08:03.380
And if you want a not gate, you string together a refuse and a collapse.

08:03.780 --> 08:04.340
Okay, hold on.

08:04.400 --> 08:05.520
You're losing me just a little bit.

08:05.520 --> 08:16.280
I understand that they map to logic gates, sure, but the document has this massive bold section at the bottom claiming that just two of these, refuse and bind, are functionally complete.

08:17.180 --> 08:19.220
What does that actually mean, like mechanically?

08:19.380 --> 08:19.560
Right.

08:19.640 --> 08:21.600
So functional completeness is a big deal.

08:21.600 --> 08:27.960
It basically means we can build the entire digital architecture of the modern world using literally nothing but those two actions.

08:28.120 --> 08:29.120
Wait, just two actions?

08:29.260 --> 08:29.520
Yes.

08:29.920 --> 08:31.040
Let's look at the mechanics.

08:31.040 --> 08:40.900
If you only have the power to invert a value, which is refuse, and the power to require two values to be true at the same time, which is bind, you can build what's called a NAND gate.

08:41.320 --> 08:42.960
NAND stands for not and D.

08:43.180 --> 08:44.200
Okay, let's trace that.

08:44.560 --> 08:47.480
How do I build a NAND gate out of just those two moves?

08:47.540 --> 08:49.420
Okay, so you take two inputs, X and Y.

08:49.580 --> 08:50.980
First, you use the bind primitive.

08:51.340 --> 08:54.800
This says I am checking if both X and DY are true.

08:55.080 --> 08:55.260
Right.

08:55.280 --> 08:58.920
Then you immediately apply the refuse primitive to that result, which inverts it.

08:58.920 --> 09:02.280
So the final output is true unless both X and Y are true.

09:02.780 --> 09:05.140
That specific combination is a NAND gate.

09:05.260 --> 09:06.780
Okay, I follow the logic there.

09:06.920 --> 09:08.740
But why is a NAND gate so special?

09:09.080 --> 09:13.680
Because in computer science, the NAND gate is like the universal building block.

09:13.900 --> 09:23.280
If you wire enough NAND gates together in different configurations, you can create an OR gate, an XOR gate, a basic memory cell, an arithmetic logic unit.

09:23.380 --> 09:24.740
And eventually a whole CPU.

09:24.740 --> 09:25.400
Exactly.

09:25.840 --> 09:27.760
Eventually a central processing unit.

09:28.300 --> 09:38.320
Every smartphone, every supercomputer, every piece of digital infrastructure on Earth operates on logic that can be entirely derived from the simple ability to bind and refuse.

09:38.540 --> 09:39.940
That is just staggering.

09:39.940 --> 09:44.920
So like, a kid playing sphere pop thinks they're just calculating that 3 minus 1 is 2.

09:45.620 --> 09:58.620
But the rules determining how they're allowed to tap the screen and how those parentheses behave are secretly hardwiring their brain to understand the exact foundational logic required to build a Turing complete computer processor.

09:58.800 --> 09:59.360
It is, yeah.

09:59.440 --> 10:03.460
It's hardcore Boolean logic disguised in primary colors and playful game mechanics.

10:03.540 --> 10:06.600
Which brings us to the massive glaring question of this deep dive.

10:06.600 --> 10:14.640
If we're talking about Turing complete logic gates and Boolean primitives, how on earth does this translate into a picture book for a literal 5-year-old?

10:14.900 --> 10:22.060
Well, to answer that, we have to actually open Flickshaneer's monograph and examine the architecture of the Sproul curriculum itself.

10:22.160 --> 10:22.940
Okay, let's do it.

10:22.960 --> 10:24.840
Because this monograph is really intimidating.

10:25.080 --> 10:27.640
I mean, it's a highly formal academic paper.

10:27.940 --> 10:34.140
It reads more like a blueprint for an operating system than like a lesson plan for a kindergarten class.

10:34.380 --> 10:35.280
It really does.

10:35.280 --> 10:42.640
So Flickshaneer's document maps out a 40-booklet progression, and it's divided into seven distinct cycles.

10:42.980 --> 10:47.280
And every single booklet is heavily constrained by what they call the three-layer architecture.

10:48.120 --> 10:50.020
All right, let's define those layers for everyone listening.

10:50.380 --> 10:51.420
What is the foundation?

10:51.940 --> 10:54.160
Okay, so layer one is historical construction.

10:54.620 --> 10:55.960
This is the sphere pop layer.

10:56.500 --> 10:59.120
It deals entirely with those primitives we just discussed.

10:59.380 --> 11:01.640
Pop, refuse, bind, collapse.

11:02.060 --> 11:04.420
At this layer, math isn't about numbers at all.

11:04.420 --> 11:09.820
It is about how a reality comes to exist in the first place through a sequence of events and choices.

11:10.080 --> 11:11.720
Okay, so the foundation isn't counting.

11:11.840 --> 11:12.780
It's tracking events.

11:13.320 --> 11:13.720
Got it.

11:13.880 --> 11:14.780
What's the second layer?

11:15.200 --> 11:17.440
Layer two is recovered structure.

11:18.040 --> 11:21.760
Now, this is the ordinary everyday mathematics that you and I would recognize.

11:22.000 --> 11:25.440
You know, counting, addition, subtraction, sets, functions.

11:25.660 --> 11:26.500
Standard school stuff.

11:26.500 --> 11:26.940
Right.

11:27.320 --> 11:32.200
But crucially, the curriculum doesn't introduce these things as pre-existing facts.

11:32.420 --> 11:37.320
It introduces them as tools that you can use to summarize the history that was built down in layer one.

11:37.460 --> 11:37.840
Oh, wow.

11:38.220 --> 11:43.920
So all the math I spent, you know, 12 years learning in school is basically just sitting in their middle layer.

11:44.120 --> 11:44.780
Pretty much, yeah.

11:45.040 --> 11:47.320
And then layer three sits on top of all that.

11:47.580 --> 11:49.040
This is epistemic transformation.

11:49.040 --> 11:53.200
And this layer deals with what you do when your mathematical map breaks.

11:53.440 --> 11:56.160
It's essentially the layer of troubleshooting reality.

11:56.600 --> 12:01.480
Like what to do when the math gives you an answer that just doesn't match the real world.

12:01.800 --> 12:02.320
Precisely.

12:02.620 --> 12:07.100
Now, if there is a central thesis to Flyxion's entire monograph like

12:07.100 --> 12:10.240
The Absolute Hill This Researcher Is Willing to Die On,

12:10.560 --> 12:14.960
it is the vital distinction between illustrating a theory and embodying a theory.

12:14.960 --> 12:19.700
Okay, we really need to linger on this because the preface gets incredibly intense about this distinction.

12:20.620 --> 12:24.220
Why is Flyxion so, I don't know, angry about illustration?

12:25.000 --> 12:31.020
Because, in their view, most modern education uses theory merely as a source of illustrations.

12:31.640 --> 12:33.960
Think about a standard science textbook, right?

12:34.240 --> 12:38.800
If the book wants to teach gravity, it shows a cartoon of an apple falling on Isaac Newton's head.

12:38.860 --> 12:39.060
Right.

12:39.220 --> 12:43.300
If a math book wants to teach logic, it draws a picture of a circuit board.

12:43.300 --> 12:47.200
But the underlying theory is just providing flavor text.

12:47.700 --> 12:52.420
If you stripped all the pictures away, the child is still just memorizing the same static equation.

12:52.720 --> 12:56.460
So the theory is essentially just clipart slapped onto rote memorization.

12:56.740 --> 12:57.100
Exactly.

12:57.240 --> 12:58.480
It's pedagogical decoration.

12:59.100 --> 13:02.240
The sprawl curriculum, however, absolutely refuses to illustrate.

13:02.760 --> 13:04.420
It demands embodiment.

13:04.820 --> 13:05.620
Meaning what, exactly?

13:05.620 --> 13:15.000
Meaning the sequence of tasks a child performs in the booklets must be a literal structural instance of the math itself being constructed in their minds.

13:15.360 --> 13:18.340
Okay, I want to try an analogy here to make sure I'm grasping the scale of this.

13:19.780 --> 13:23.980
Illustration is like building a modern house, but you want it to look like a medieval castle.

13:23.980 --> 13:31.760
So you frame it out with cheap two-by-fours, put up some drywall, and then you just glue some faux stone plastic siding to the outside.

13:32.180 --> 13:35.240
You know, maybe hang a fake plastic sword in the hallway.

13:35.840 --> 13:36.600
It looks like a castle.

13:36.800 --> 13:38.960
But underneath, it's just a cheap modern frame.

13:39.720 --> 13:45.400
Embodiment, on the other hand, is forcing the builders to go to the quarry, cut 12th century stone blocks,

13:45.720 --> 13:51.900
and actually use medieval masonry techniques to build load-bearing walls from the ground up without power tools.

13:51.900 --> 13:55.080
That analogy hits the nail right on the head.

13:55.680 --> 14:01.320
If a storm hits the modern house and rips off that faux stone siding, the house still stands,

14:01.700 --> 14:03.680
because the castle part was just an illusion.

14:04.140 --> 14:07.980
But in an embodied structure, the castle is the structure.

14:08.380 --> 14:08.480
Wow.

14:08.780 --> 14:14.160
Yeah, and Flyxion provides a very specific, rigid example of how this constraint dictates the curriculum.

14:14.700 --> 14:20.440
They state that a child cannot learn the concept of collapse, which, remember, is evaluating a final result.

14:20.440 --> 14:22.420
They can't learn that in booklet number four.

14:22.840 --> 14:23.520
Wait, why not?

14:23.640 --> 14:23.820
Yeah.

14:23.920 --> 14:27.260
Is collapse just too complicated for a kindergartner to understand?

14:27.460 --> 14:28.180
Not at all.

14:28.360 --> 14:31.660
A child can easily understand the idea of finishing a puzzle.

14:32.460 --> 14:37.980
But formally, mathematically, nothing in the child's constructed experience up to booklet four

14:37.980 --> 14:44.360
has yet distinguished between a rule for observing something and the history of the thing being observed.

14:44.640 --> 14:44.960
Oh.

14:44.960 --> 14:52.080
Yeah, because the child hasn't built that architectural wall yet, the concept of collapse literally cannot exist on the page.

14:52.620 --> 14:53.320
That's insane.

14:53.420 --> 14:59.380
So the authors literally cannot put the lesson on the page because the mathematical universe they are building inside the child's mind

14:59.380 --> 15:01.940
hasn't invented that law of physics yet.

15:02.040 --> 15:02.220
Right.

15:02.300 --> 15:03.500
The universe doesn't support it.

15:03.500 --> 15:03.980
Exactly.

15:03.980 --> 15:08.660
The ordering of the Sproul booklets is not based on what is easiest for a teacher to explain.

15:09.040 --> 15:16.320
It is based on the exact, uncompromising order that mathematical logic imposes on what is allowed to be stated before something else.

15:16.400 --> 15:19.500
Which leads directly into the slogan of the entire series.

15:19.720 --> 15:24.180
It's printed right in the visual guide, and I honestly want it tattooed on my arm.

15:24.220 --> 15:25.060
It's a great slogan.

15:25.340 --> 15:25.780
It is.

15:25.940 --> 15:29.260
The slogan is, don't erase what you learned, learn what made it true.

15:29.260 --> 15:34.060
And that phrase summarizes their formal principle of monotonic learning.

15:34.940 --> 15:37.740
It is a strict structural mandate for the authors.

15:38.700 --> 15:48.040
If a booklet makes a claim in cycle one, a later booklet in cycle seven is never, ever allowed to say that the earlier claim was false.

15:48.200 --> 15:52.980
Which is honestly the most deeply frustrating part of traditional education.

15:52.980 --> 15:59.440
I remember sitting in like third grade, and the teacher said, you cannot subtract a bigger number from a smaller number.

15:59.600 --> 16:01.540
You can't do three minus five.

16:02.140 --> 16:03.620
It's impossible.

16:04.040 --> 16:05.180
We all learn that, yeah.

16:05.260 --> 16:05.460
Right.

16:05.560 --> 16:06.760
I internalize that rule.

16:06.880 --> 16:07.100
Yeah.

16:07.240 --> 16:11.620
Then I get to middle school, and the new teacher says, actually, you can, it's negative two.

16:11.860 --> 16:13.800
What you learn in elementary school is a lie.

16:14.100 --> 16:14.860
Oh, exactly.

16:14.880 --> 16:15.500
Or in chemistry.

16:15.740 --> 16:15.900
Yeah.

16:15.920 --> 16:19.720
They teach you, atoms are like little solar systems with electrons on neat little racetracks.

16:19.720 --> 16:23.700
Then you get to high school physics, and they say, actually, that was a gross oversimplification.

16:24.040 --> 16:25.180
They were probability clouds.

16:25.780 --> 16:30.460
You spend half your academic life frantically unlearning the convenient lies they told you in the previous grade.

16:30.700 --> 16:34.780
And from an epistemic standpoint, that creates profound distrust in the learner.

16:35.480 --> 16:43.080
The student subconsciously starts to assume that whatever they are currently being taught is probably just another placeholder lie just waiting to be overwritten by the next teacher.

16:43.300 --> 16:43.480
Yeah.

16:44.060 --> 16:45.960
Monotonic learning prevents that trauma.

16:45.960 --> 16:51.800
If a child learns a truth in Sproul A01, that truth remains solid rock forever.

16:52.580 --> 16:58.980
A later booklet might reveal that the earlier truth was specific to a narrow set of rules, and now we are expanding the rules.

16:59.220 --> 17:02.660
But it never, ever erases the foundational truth.

17:03.140 --> 17:05.660
It just treats the learner's mind with such profound respect.

17:06.220 --> 17:06.760
I love that.

17:06.840 --> 17:10.620
So I want to see exactly how this embodiment works in practice.

17:10.940 --> 17:14.600
Let's open the very first books a child sees in cycle one.

17:14.600 --> 17:17.880
And this is the pre-math layer, the literal beginning of existence.

17:18.360 --> 17:19.560
Let's look at Sproul 00.

17:19.780 --> 17:19.920
Okay.

17:20.040 --> 17:22.340
So the title of Sproul 00 is A Difference.

17:22.480 --> 17:25.120
And the subtitle printed underneath is A Book Before the Beginning.

17:25.440 --> 17:30.780
I noticed in the monograph that Flyxion is very proud of the fact that this booklet contains absolutely zero arithmetic.

17:31.300 --> 17:32.060
There are no numbers.

17:32.320 --> 17:33.720
It doesn't even use the word pop.

17:33.880 --> 17:40.380
Its singular goal is to force the child to practice the one cognitive act that sits underneath all logic.

17:40.380 --> 17:47.180
Right, which is individuation, the act of noticing that a specific thing can be told apart from the background noise of the universe.

17:47.400 --> 17:49.680
Let's walk through the imagery because it is so stark.

17:50.200 --> 17:51.800
Page two is mostly empty space.

17:51.960 --> 17:55.340
There is just a single, small, solid black dot right in the center.

17:55.900 --> 17:57.800
The text simply says, here is something.

17:57.980 --> 17:58.600
You turn the page.

17:59.020 --> 18:01.060
Page three shows a small orange triangle.

18:01.580 --> 18:03.080
The text says, here is something else.

18:03.260 --> 18:05.200
It establishes presence, very basic.

18:05.400 --> 18:09.280
But then it rapidly expands the parameter of what something is allowed to be.

18:09.280 --> 18:11.240
Right, page four isn't a geometric shape.

18:11.440 --> 18:15.140
It's a line drawing of two hands clapping with little vibration lines.

18:15.620 --> 18:17.740
The text says, a loud clap is something.

18:18.400 --> 18:20.740
Page five is just three faint rippling rings.

18:21.100 --> 18:22.600
A quiet sound is something too.

18:23.080 --> 18:24.600
Page six has no object at all.

18:24.680 --> 18:29.080
It's just a soft gray shadow cast onto the ground by something off page.

18:29.540 --> 18:31.180
A shadow is something too.

18:31.620 --> 18:35.480
And then page seven is just a massive, violent, chaotic scribble of ink.

18:35.840 --> 18:37.880
A big, complicated scribble is something.

18:37.880 --> 18:39.980
And then the book synthesizes the whole concept.

18:40.160 --> 18:42.200
It displays all these disparate items in a row.

18:42.300 --> 18:44.980
The dot, the triangle, the clap, the shadow, the scribble.

18:45.100 --> 18:46.520
They're all scaled to the same size.

18:46.820 --> 18:49.620
And the text reads, something does not mean big.

18:49.740 --> 18:50.920
Something does not mean loud.

18:51.080 --> 18:52.360
Something does not mean round.

18:52.980 --> 18:55.220
Something just means, here it is.

18:55.280 --> 18:56.980
Something just means, here it is.

18:57.280 --> 18:58.320
That is incredibly powerful.

18:58.640 --> 19:03.800
It strips away all the attributes we usually use to define objects and just leaves the pure fact of existence.

19:03.800 --> 19:09.220
It is deeply ontological, but once they establish existence, they immediately introduce complication.

19:09.580 --> 19:12.340
The next page shows two small black dots.

19:12.720 --> 19:15.560
They are visually identical, placed side by side.

19:16.080 --> 19:18.040
The text says, they look alike.

19:18.580 --> 19:19.560
You turn the page.

19:19.960 --> 19:24.560
It's the same two identical dots, but now they are placed on opposite far corners of the page.

19:24.720 --> 19:27.180
And the book asks a question, are they the same?

19:27.180 --> 19:30.680
And here's where I got genuinely confused when I was reading the source materials.

19:31.740 --> 19:33.220
The book doesn't answer the question.

19:33.860 --> 19:36.560
There is no turn the page to find out.

19:36.660 --> 19:38.280
There's no answer key at the back.

19:38.460 --> 19:43.860
It just leaves this massive philosophical question hanging in the air for a five-year-old.

19:44.360 --> 19:45.700
Why would you do that?

19:46.020 --> 19:48.480
Because answering it would violate the architecture.

19:48.700 --> 19:48.920
Really?

19:49.100 --> 19:49.400
Yes.

19:49.680 --> 19:54.520
The monograph lists intentional constraint number four from the curriculum's house style, which states,

19:54.520 --> 19:59.020
an unresolved formal question must not be given a resolved illustration.

19:59.880 --> 20:04.360
The note for grown-ups printed in the back of the booklet explicitly warns parents and teachers

20:04.360 --> 20:06.220
not to answer the question for the child.

20:06.440 --> 20:08.580
So they want the kid to just be slightly confused.

20:08.880 --> 20:12.480
The cognitive friction of sitting with the unknown is the entire point of the lesson.

20:12.800 --> 20:15.160
And the book does it again on the very next page.

20:15.840 --> 20:22.120
It draws a big circle around a busy cluster of items, a dot, a triangle, a scribble, all grouped

20:22.120 --> 20:23.280
together inside this boundary.

20:23.280 --> 20:27.320
The text asks, can many things be treated as one?

20:28.060 --> 20:33.200
The next page shows the exact same image, but places a giant question mark over it.

20:33.780 --> 20:38.120
The text reads, sometimes, but what do we mean by one?

20:38.600 --> 20:42.720
And if we connect this back to the mathematical primitives we discussed earlier, we can see

20:42.720 --> 20:44.860
exactly why the book refuses to answer.

20:44.980 --> 20:45.820
Oh, the primitives.

20:45.940 --> 20:46.780
How does that connect?

20:46.780 --> 20:52.180
Well, asking, can many things be treated as one, is poking at the fundamental difference

20:52.180 --> 20:57.360
between coupling things together, which is the bind primitive, and permanently evaluating

20:57.360 --> 21:00.140
them into a single output, which is the collapse primitive.

21:00.440 --> 21:00.740
Ooh.

21:01.140 --> 21:01.440
Yeah.

21:01.560 --> 21:05.700
If the authors gave the child an answer right now, before the concepts of bind and collapse

21:05.700 --> 21:09.780
have been formally built in the child's mind, they would be skipping a step in the construction

21:09.780 --> 21:10.460
of reality.

21:10.620 --> 21:13.640
They would be gluing the faux stone siding onto the house.

21:13.640 --> 21:14.200
Exactly.

21:14.820 --> 21:18.080
The foundation just isn't ready to bear the weight of that answer yet.

21:18.540 --> 21:19.160
Okay, I see you now.

21:19.560 --> 21:25.940
So, Sproul 00 ends by displaying three clean, plain shapes on a page.

21:26.400 --> 21:28.100
A circle, a triangle, and a square.

21:28.720 --> 21:31.160
The final text says, here are three things, something could happen.

21:31.640 --> 21:34.200
And that cliffhanger leads us directly into Sproul 01.

21:34.500 --> 21:34.740
Right.

21:34.860 --> 21:38.480
And Sproul 01 is titled, Something Happened, The First Pop.

21:38.480 --> 21:40.540
We open on those same three shapes.

21:40.700 --> 21:42.300
The text says, there are three ways to go.

21:42.440 --> 21:45.760
You turn the page, and a little cartoon hand is hovering over the shapes.

21:46.040 --> 21:46.540
Choose one.

21:47.180 --> 21:51.860
Next page, the square is now drawn in a thick, bold, black line, while the circle and the

21:51.860 --> 21:54.860
triangle have faded into these faint, ghostly gray outlines.

21:55.140 --> 21:57.760
The text declares, you chose the square.

21:58.480 --> 22:00.240
And then it drops the vocabulary word.

22:00.600 --> 22:01.500
That choice is a pop.

22:01.660 --> 22:03.440
This is a monumental transition.

22:03.440 --> 22:07.880
We have moved from merely observing the fact that things are different, to an agent taking

22:07.880 --> 22:10.120
an action that permanently alters the state of the board.

22:10.420 --> 22:13.860
The monograph is incredibly pedantic about the formal definition here, by the way.

22:14.100 --> 22:14.480
How so?

22:14.740 --> 22:16.660
Well, a pop is not the square itself.

22:16.740 --> 22:17.960
It is not the ink on the page.

22:18.160 --> 22:19.960
A pop is the invisible verb.

22:20.320 --> 22:23.580
It is the act of choosing one path from a set of available paths.

22:23.920 --> 22:26.100
It is the destruction of alternative possibilities.

22:26.680 --> 22:30.420
The adult note at the back of Sproul 01 points this out.

22:30.480 --> 22:32.900
It says, before the choice, there were three ways to go.

22:33.640 --> 22:38.000
After it, one history exists, and the other two ways of going stayed unchosen.

22:38.820 --> 22:43.920
This is where the curriculum introduces my absolute favorite visual device, the ledger

22:43.920 --> 22:44.140
strip.

22:44.300 --> 22:45.040
Ah, yes.

22:45.280 --> 22:47.900
The ledger strip is the engine of the entire curriculum.

22:48.300 --> 22:52.060
It looks like a single horizontal line cut out of a lined notebook.

22:52.680 --> 22:57.780
After the child pops the square, a tiny drawing of a square appears inside this ledger strip

22:57.780 --> 22:58.980
at the bottom of the page.

22:59.060 --> 23:01.200
The text says, now we have a history.

23:01.200 --> 23:03.140
Then the book resets the experiment.

23:03.580 --> 23:04.260
Fresh page.

23:04.700 --> 23:05.780
Three clean shapes again.

23:06.000 --> 23:06.580
Let's try again.

23:06.640 --> 23:07.620
This time, choose the triangle.

23:07.940 --> 23:10.580
We get the bold triangle and the faded circle and square.

23:11.020 --> 23:14.720
But now, we look down at the bottom of the page and we see two ledger strips stacked on

23:14.720 --> 23:15.360
top of each other.

23:15.520 --> 23:17.160
The top strip has a square inside it.

23:17.360 --> 23:18.600
The bottom strip has a triangle.

23:18.920 --> 23:20.640
The text says, now there are two histories.

23:20.640 --> 23:23.520
What is happening mechanically here is profound.

23:24.200 --> 23:28.240
The book is showing the child that you can start with the exact same initial conditions,

23:28.660 --> 23:34.100
but end up with completely different, equally legitimate realities based entirely on the

23:34.100 --> 23:35.340
history of choices that were made.

23:35.700 --> 23:41.560
And this leads to an absolute aha moment at the end of Sproul 01.

23:41.560 --> 23:47.240
The book finally introduces the concept of counting, but it does it in a way that feels

23:47.240 --> 23:49.520
completely alien to how I learned numbers.

23:49.840 --> 23:50.420
How so?

23:50.620 --> 23:55.060
Well, it shows a ledger strip containing two items, first a circle, then a filled triangle.

23:55.440 --> 23:57.580
The book asks, how many pops happened?

23:57.980 --> 24:00.600
And the text provides the answer, two pops happened.

24:00.720 --> 24:01.040
Right.

24:01.160 --> 24:05.400
This is the exact moment the Sproul curriculum divorces itself from traditional mathematics.

24:05.400 --> 24:06.700
So what does this all mean?

24:06.840 --> 24:10.960
Like, why does the curriculum refuse to just draw three apples on a page and tell the kid

24:10.960 --> 24:12.380
to point at them and say, one, two, three?

24:12.780 --> 24:16.220
Why are they defining counting as measuring the length of a history of choices?

24:16.800 --> 24:22.320
Because if you just point at three apples on a page, you are reinforcing the x-ray model

24:22.320 --> 24:22.940
of math.

24:23.520 --> 24:29.200
You are teaching the child that threeness is a magical, invisible property that lives inside

24:29.200 --> 24:32.340
the apples completely independent of the human observing them.

24:32.600 --> 24:33.800
Which is how we all learn it.

24:33.880 --> 24:34.100
Right.

24:34.100 --> 24:38.800
But the sphere pop calculus operates on the premise that existence is historical, not

24:38.800 --> 24:39.480
axiomatic.

24:40.280 --> 24:42.600
A number isn't a property floating in space.

24:43.200 --> 24:46.620
A number is a strict accounting of committed choices.

24:46.920 --> 24:49.480
You aren't counting things, you're counting events.

24:49.600 --> 24:50.320
Yes, exactly.

24:50.760 --> 24:54.280
If you write a history out in mathematical notation, it looks like a sequence.

24:54.540 --> 24:59.160
History H equals event one, followed by event two, all the way to event N.

24:59.700 --> 25:01.800
The child is pounding the length of that sequence.

25:01.800 --> 25:07.180
The monograph explicitly states that they do not want the child to generalize what two means

25:07.180 --> 25:07.500
yet.

25:07.760 --> 25:10.260
They don't want them thinking about two dogs or two cars.

25:10.460 --> 25:14.600
They want the child locked into the understanding that two is the number of times a decision

25:14.600 --> 25:15.100
was made.

25:15.100 --> 25:19.480
Which sets the stage perfectly for the central conflict of Sproul 02.

25:19.480 --> 25:22.480
The title is, What Happened First?

25:22.480 --> 25:27.020
And this is the booklet where the philosophical implications just hit you like a freight train.

25:27.020 --> 25:31.740
Let's walk through the central experiment of Sproul 02, because it proves exactly why counting

25:31.740 --> 25:32.980
events is so crucial.

25:32.980 --> 25:34.760
Okay, we start with a clean page.

25:35.300 --> 25:37.420
We pop a circle, it becomes solid.

25:37.780 --> 25:42.460
Then we make a second pop, choosing a triangle, and we place it next to the circle.

25:42.820 --> 25:47.680
So the final image is a plain circle on the left and a plain triangle on the right.

25:48.220 --> 25:50.920
The text says, here is what we have now.

25:50.920 --> 25:52.880
Okay, so that is history number one.

25:53.060 --> 25:53.180
Right.

25:53.720 --> 25:56.620
Then the book runs the simulation again, clean page.

25:57.280 --> 26:00.740
But this time, we pop the triangle first, it becomes solid.

26:01.000 --> 26:02.620
Then we pop the circle next to it.

26:03.080 --> 26:06.340
The book arranges the final shapes so they match the first picture perfectly.

26:06.760 --> 26:09.240
A circle on the left, a triangle on the right.

26:09.360 --> 26:10.380
And that's history number two.

26:10.660 --> 26:14.760
The book places the two final pictures side by side on the page.

26:14.960 --> 26:17.120
They're visually identical in every single way.

26:17.120 --> 26:19.180
The text asks, are these the same?

26:19.520 --> 26:21.000
And the book answers its own question.

26:21.360 --> 26:23.080
Yes, they look exactly the same.

26:23.320 --> 26:25.920
But then it introduces the epistemic friction.

26:26.380 --> 26:26.620
Yes.

26:27.120 --> 26:31.940
It puts a giant question mark over the identical pictures and asks, but which happened first?

26:32.280 --> 26:33.280
The picture doesn't say.

26:33.840 --> 26:36.680
Then it directs the child's attention down to the ledger strips.

26:37.020 --> 26:40.260
The first ledger strip reads, circle, then triangle.

26:40.640 --> 26:43.980
The second ledger strip reads, triangle, then circle.

26:44.620 --> 26:46.580
The text delivers the final lesson.

26:47.180 --> 26:48.180
The history remembers.

26:48.800 --> 26:51.420
Even when the picture doesn't, same things, different history.

26:51.960 --> 26:55.460
This is the formal introduction of what Flyxion calls historical identity.

26:56.220 --> 27:00.760
It establishes a foundational truth that honestly, many adults struggle to grasp.

27:01.520 --> 27:06.880
Two identical looking outcomes are fundamentally not the same thing if the histories that generated

27:06.880 --> 27:07.500
them are different.

27:07.820 --> 27:10.040
I mean, think about how this applies to real world logic.

27:10.180 --> 27:10.400
Right.

27:10.620 --> 27:14.580
Imagine you were looking at two people who both have exactly $1 million sitting in their

27:14.580 --> 27:15.060
bank accounts.

27:15.060 --> 27:19.680
The picture, the final collapsed state of their finances, is identical.

27:20.180 --> 27:26.300
But if person A won the lottery yesterday and person B saved 10% of their paycheck from

27:26.300 --> 27:30.080
a blue-collar job for 40 years, those are not the same million dollars.

27:30.080 --> 27:36.200
The history behind the money changes the fundamental nature of the reality you are interacting with.

27:36.640 --> 27:40.520
If you treat them as identical just because the final number is the same, you are going to make

27:40.520 --> 27:42.240
terrible assumptions about those two people.

27:42.240 --> 27:45.220
That is the exact trap the curriculum is trying to avoid.

27:45.860 --> 27:48.820
A picture, or a final collapsed state, is just a summary.

27:49.440 --> 27:53.620
And a summary, by its very mathematical definition, must discard information.

27:54.260 --> 27:56.840
By erasing the timeline, it loses the truth.

27:57.320 --> 28:02.340
The curriculum is hardwiring the child to understand that you can never fully trust a final picture.

28:02.560 --> 28:07.820
In the monograph, Flyxion explicitly ties this lesson back to the collapse primitive we talked

28:07.820 --> 28:08.860
about with the logic gates.

28:08.860 --> 28:12.420
Yes, the formal concept of permanently evaluating a state.

28:12.880 --> 28:17.160
Flyxion explains that the child is learning that they have a choice in how they observe the world.

28:17.740 --> 28:22.480
You can apply a collapse rule to look at the picture, which means you choose to ignore the ledger

28:22.480 --> 28:24.200
and just look at the final shapes.

28:24.940 --> 28:27.600
Under that specific rule, the two histories are equal.

28:27.900 --> 28:28.100
Right.

28:28.220 --> 28:33.560
Or, you can apply a collapse rule to look at the sequence, which means you read the ledger.

28:34.320 --> 28:36.940
Under that rule, the two histories are completely unequal.

28:36.940 --> 28:38.940
And here is the kicker.

28:39.880 --> 28:43.540
Flyxion insists that neither rule is more correct than the other.

28:43.960 --> 28:45.380
They just answer different questions.

28:45.860 --> 28:47.980
It builds incredible cognitive flexibility.

28:48.180 --> 28:51.240
The child learns that equality is not a universal truth.

28:51.940 --> 28:56.780
Equality is entirely dependent on which collapse rule you decide to apply to the data.

28:57.020 --> 28:57.900
It is brilliant.

28:58.360 --> 29:00.280
So, we've spent a lot of time in cycle one.

29:00.280 --> 29:06.140
We understand that math is a recorded history of choices and that the ledger is more important than the picture.

29:06.560 --> 29:07.700
But how does this scale?

29:08.080 --> 29:12.520
Wait, if a number is just a history of choices, how on earth do you do basic addition?

29:12.820 --> 29:13.880
Ah, I see where you're going.

29:14.020 --> 29:18.160
Like, if I have two apples and I add three apples, where are the choices in that?

29:18.480 --> 29:20.980
Does this whole system just break down when we hit basic arithmetic?

29:21.400 --> 29:23.520
Let's look at the middle cycles, cycles two through three.

29:23.520 --> 29:25.500
Well, the system doesn't break down.

29:25.620 --> 29:27.720
It redefines the operations entirely.

29:28.560 --> 29:34.220
In these middle cycles, we transition from the historical construction layer into the recovered structure layer.

29:34.500 --> 29:39.440
We are finally building the math you recognize, but we are building it exclusively out of pops and ledgers.

29:39.880 --> 29:42.900
To answer your question about addition, we have to look at cycle three.

29:43.260 --> 29:46.140
But before we hit arithmetic, we have to talk about cycle two, right?

29:46.180 --> 29:46.360
Yeah.

29:46.400 --> 29:47.940
Because cycle two sets up the boundaries.

29:48.100 --> 29:50.580
It's called rules, making little worlds.

29:50.580 --> 29:51.260
Yes.

29:51.840 --> 29:55.300
Cycle two introduces the mathematical concept of admissibility.

29:56.160 --> 29:58.580
What is allowed to exist in a specific space?

29:59.220 --> 30:01.860
In Sproul 11, they introduce the box.

30:02.200 --> 30:04.920
The child pops various shapes and colors into existence.

30:05.180 --> 30:07.920
Some of them are allowed inside the box, and some of them bounce off.

30:08.160 --> 30:13.520
But the genius of Sproul 12, the rule of the box, is that it doesn't give the child the rule up front.

30:14.000 --> 30:19.340
The child has to forensically discover the membership rule after the fact by looking at what made it inside.

30:19.340 --> 30:25.660
Exactly. The child might notice that a red circle and a red square are in the box, but a blue circle is outside.

30:26.160 --> 30:29.660
They have to deduce that the rule is only red things.

30:30.160 --> 30:39.280
It establishes that a mathematical space, what mathematicians call a domain or an omega, is defined by its governing rule, not just by listing the objects inside it.

30:39.360 --> 30:42.040
Which is exactly how we discover rules in real life.

30:42.500 --> 30:46.480
You start a new job, nobody hands you a comprehensive list of office politics.

30:46.560 --> 30:47.300
No, they don't.

30:47.300 --> 30:53.300
You just observe that when Dave speaks up in a meeting, he gets praised, but when Sarah speaks up, she gets ignored.

30:53.780 --> 30:59.140
You observe the outcomes, and you reverse engineer the invisible rule of that specific box.

30:59.420 --> 31:02.600
And that reverse engineering is exactly what Cycle 3 applies to addition.

31:02.900 --> 31:05.400
Cycle 3 is called paths, arithmetic as movement.

31:05.840 --> 31:07.260
Okay, let's tackle my Apple problem.

31:07.620 --> 31:09.080
How does Sproul handle plus 1?

31:09.080 --> 31:15.560
In Sproul 16, which is titled Take a Step, plus 1 and minus 1 are not introduced as static amounts.

31:16.060 --> 31:18.420
They are introduced as transitions between states.

31:18.840 --> 31:21.580
They are pop events that occur on a number line history.

31:21.920 --> 31:25.540
So arithmetic isn't about combining piles of static objects.

31:25.720 --> 31:27.080
It is about taking a journey.

31:27.640 --> 31:28.780
Addition is a path.

31:28.780 --> 31:29.340
Exactly.

31:30.120 --> 31:32.380
Sproul 17 is called Many Ways to 5.

31:32.880 --> 31:38.340
It visually demonstrates that the number 5 can be reached by a history of taking 4 steps and adding 1,

31:38.460 --> 31:43.240
or taking 3 steps and adding 2, or taking 7 steps and walking backward 2 steps.

31:43.340 --> 31:45.880
Oh, this is a direct echo of Sproul 02.

31:46.720 --> 31:48.880
Different histories leading to the identical picture.

31:49.540 --> 31:52.980
Different arithmetic paths leading to the identical collapse value of 5.

31:52.980 --> 31:58.760
The path to the result becomes the actual object being studied, rather than just memorizing the result.

31:59.040 --> 32:03.580
And that focus on the process sets up the most conceptually ambitious part of the middle cycles.

32:03.840 --> 32:07.240
Cycle 4, which is titled Machines, Functions, and Composition.

32:07.520 --> 32:10.720
Functions are basically introduced as machines that transform pops.

32:11.240 --> 32:17.040
In Sproul 21, the number machine, a child puts a 2 into a slot, the machine rumbles, and a 4 pops out.

32:17.220 --> 32:21.660
The child's job is to figure out what mathematical rule the machine used to change the input.

32:21.660 --> 32:31.400
But the absolute standout of Cycle 4, the booklet that Flyxion dedicates an enormous amount of space to in the monograph, is Sproul 24.

32:32.120 --> 32:35.660
It is titled Machines That Forget and What We Can Get Back.

32:36.540 --> 32:39.400
I read the summary of this in the source material, and I have to ask,

32:40.160 --> 32:44.460
are they seriously teaching 5-year-olds about entropy and information theory?

32:44.540 --> 32:46.300
Because that feels absurdly advanced.

32:46.460 --> 32:48.820
They absolutely are, and they are doing it seamlessly.

32:48.820 --> 32:53.780
Flyxion notes that originally this topic was going to be split across two separate booklets.

32:54.340 --> 32:58.380
But the authors realize that the concept of a machine losing information doesn't matter,

32:58.800 --> 33:01.460
unless you also ask whether that information can be recovered.

33:01.800 --> 33:02.860
They have to be taught together.

33:02.980 --> 33:05.980
Okay, walk me through the mechanics of this forgetful machine in the book.

33:06.160 --> 33:07.740
The book presents a coloring puzzle.

33:08.080 --> 33:09.780
The child is introduced to a new machine.

33:10.280 --> 33:14.340
If you feed a red ball into the machine, it paints it purple and spits it out.

33:14.480 --> 33:15.020
Spible enough.

33:15.020 --> 33:19.980
But if you feed a blue ball into the same machine, it also paints it purple and spits it out.

33:20.440 --> 33:22.800
Mathematically, this is known as a many-to-one function.

33:23.320 --> 33:26.500
Two distinct inputs lead to the exact same output.

33:27.000 --> 33:31.080
The machine intentionally destroys the distinction between red and blue.

33:31.440 --> 33:32.760
It loses information.

33:32.760 --> 33:36.780
And the booklet immediately pivots the child's focus.

33:37.280 --> 33:44.440
Instead of asking, what color does the machine make, the text points to a purple ball sitting in the output tray and asks,

33:44.700 --> 33:49.220
if the machine spits out purple, can you ever know for sure what color you put in?

33:49.920 --> 33:50.780
And the answer is no.

33:51.480 --> 33:53.540
Because it could have been red, or it could have been blue.

33:54.280 --> 33:57.080
The history has been permanently erased by the machine's rule.

33:57.400 --> 34:00.240
You cannot deterministically recover the original input.

34:00.240 --> 34:07.800
It is a profound lesson on the irreversibility of certain processes, completely disguised as a puzzle about paint colors.

34:08.280 --> 34:10.800
Thinking about this as a learner, it is just so refreshing.

34:11.200 --> 34:15.140
Instead of agonizing over memorizing a multiplication table through brute force,

34:15.520 --> 34:20.240
you are literally being taught how to reverse engineer a system to see if its history is recoverable.

34:20.440 --> 34:21.340
You aren't a calculator.

34:21.540 --> 34:22.680
You're a mathematical detective.

34:22.920 --> 34:27.640
It shifts the burden of math from rote memorization to forensic investigation.

34:27.640 --> 34:36.580
But all of this, all of this beautiful, rigid, logical structure about ledgers and boxes and forgetful machines

34:36.580 --> 34:40.340
is really just setting the stage for the climax of the curriculum.

34:40.680 --> 34:45.660
Because Cycle 6 is where Sproul decides to intentionally break the universe it just spent years building.

34:45.840 --> 34:48.000
Cycle 6 is titled Broken Maps.

34:48.000 --> 34:53.680
And this brings us to the third and final layer of Flyxion's architecture, the epistemic layer.

34:54.120 --> 34:58.880
This section is heavily drawn from a referenced essay called Beyond Prediction Error.

34:59.360 --> 35:05.720
I want to talk about Sproul 29 because reading the description of this booklet felt like an act of pedagogical rebellion.

35:06.400 --> 35:12.320
The book deliberately presents the child with a puzzle where every single available multiple choice answer is wrong.

35:12.440 --> 35:15.980
It violently violates the unstated social contract of textbook learning.

35:15.980 --> 35:19.340
Every student is conditioned to believe that if they are handed a multiple choice question,

35:19.540 --> 35:23.460
the correct answer must be one of the options provided by the authority figure.

35:23.780 --> 35:26.700
But in real life, outside of a classroom, that is almost never true.

35:27.380 --> 35:31.600
Sometimes the spreadsheet is corrupted, the data is bad, and none of the options work.

35:32.480 --> 35:36.120
Sproul 29 forces the child to confront the panic of a broken reality.

35:36.840 --> 35:40.360
And then Sproul 30 swoops in to teach them how to fix it.

35:40.560 --> 35:42.120
It's called Make a New Answer.

35:42.120 --> 35:49.740
It literally instructs the child to take a pencil, draw a bigger box on the page, and enlarge the space of allowed possibilities.

35:50.320 --> 35:53.760
This raises a vital question for the underlying formal calculus.

35:54.260 --> 36:00.580
When the map breaks, how do we mathematically categorize the different ways it failed and the different tools we can use to fix it?

36:00.860 --> 36:06.480
The monograph introduces four specific epistemic operators for this task, U, Q, P, and A.

36:06.480 --> 36:10.660
Okay, we need to dive deep into these mechanics because this is the core of the epistemic layer.

36:11.020 --> 36:13.820
Walk me through a scenario where a child has to use these operators.

36:14.040 --> 36:15.200
Let's start with U and Q.

36:15.500 --> 36:22.520
Let's imagine a child is trying to solve a puzzle where they have to guess which shape is hidden inside a box based on a few clues.

36:23.360 --> 36:25.240
U stands for revising a commitment.

36:26.020 --> 36:29.960
Let's say the child guesses triangle, but the book reveals they are wrong.

36:29.960 --> 36:39.620
Applying the U operator means the child simply drops their guess of triangle, looks at the remaining options on the page, say, a circle and a square, and guesses again.

36:39.900 --> 36:43.260
They are just updating their choice within the existing unbroken map.

36:43.880 --> 36:46.240
Okay, so they just made a bad guess, so the guess again.

36:46.380 --> 36:46.660
Simple.

36:47.060 --> 36:48.000
What about Q?

36:48.360 --> 36:50.560
Q stands for seeking different evidence.

36:50.920 --> 36:53.620
The child realizes that guessing randomly isn't working.

36:53.820 --> 36:54.620
They need more data.

36:54.620 --> 37:01.820
So applying the Q operator means the child actively looks for a new clue on the page that they missed, maybe a shadow that hints at a round edge.

37:02.200 --> 37:05.640
They haven't changed the map, but they are changing how they interact with the evidence.

37:06.040 --> 37:08.020
Okay, those two make intuitive sense.

37:08.220 --> 37:09.700
You guess again, or you look closer.

37:10.220 --> 37:14.700
But the next two operators, P and A, are where the curriculum takes off the training wheels.

37:15.420 --> 37:19.640
Let's talk about P, reweighting, and A, admissibility revision.

37:19.640 --> 37:30.960
Flyxion spends an entire chapter, chapter 6, getting incredibly excited about how these operators create what they call the three-fold non-equivalence.

37:31.160 --> 37:33.580
Yes, this is the crown jewel of cycle 6.

37:34.140 --> 37:42.380
The curriculum teaches the child that seeing a crossed-out shape on a piece of paper can mean three entirely different things, both mathematically and philosophically.

37:42.800 --> 37:47.560
And knowing the difference between those three marks is the absolute key to critical thinking.

37:47.740 --> 37:49.320
Let's trace this mechanically.

37:49.640 --> 37:51.860
Walk us through the three different ways to cross out a shape.

37:51.860 --> 37:54.580
The first type of crossing out is zero-weighting.

37:54.800 --> 37:55.980
This utilizes operator P.

37:56.420 --> 37:59.760
You see a shape on the page, and the child writes a weight of zero next to it.

38:00.440 --> 38:04.860
This translates to, I have considered this option, but based on my current evidence, I do not trust it.

38:05.200 --> 38:09.880
The option is still technically on the map, it just has zero influence on the decision right now.

38:10.000 --> 38:10.820
So it's sidelined.

38:10.900 --> 38:13.160
It's sitting on the bench, but it's still on the team roster.

38:13.520 --> 38:13.960
Exactly.

38:14.600 --> 38:16.580
The second type of crossing out is refusal.

38:16.580 --> 38:22.140
This calls all the way back to the R primitive from the sphere-pop logic gates we discussed at the beginning.

38:22.640 --> 38:25.960
You see a shape struck through with a specific historical mark beside it.

38:26.340 --> 38:30.420
This translates to, I have tested this, and I have documented it as a hard no.

38:30.640 --> 38:31.400
It is refused.

38:31.860 --> 38:35.860
But crucially, it remains permanently inscribed in the historical ledger.

38:35.860 --> 38:41.040
You can always point to that mark and say, we definitely tried that path, and it definitively failed.

38:41.540 --> 38:43.300
It is part of the permanent historical record.

38:43.460 --> 38:46.600
You don't erase it because the failure itself is valuable data.

38:46.760 --> 38:47.020
Yes.

38:47.360 --> 38:53.660
And the third type of crossing out is domain revision, specifically called compraction, denoted mathematically as A-.

38:53.660 --> 39:02.480
This is when a shape is not just crossed out, but the actual boundary line drawn around the options is erased and redrawn smaller, completely excluding the shape.

39:02.740 --> 39:06.560
This translates to, this shape is no longer a candidate at all.

39:06.680 --> 39:07.420
It was an illusion.

39:07.700 --> 39:10.380
It does not exist in our space of possible realities.

39:10.380 --> 39:19.760
Here's where it gets really interesting, because distinguishing between those three specific operations is vital for surviving the real world.

39:20.340 --> 39:22.480
Let's try to map this to a real-life failure.

39:23.080 --> 39:25.800
Let's talk about using a GPS navigation system.

39:25.960 --> 39:27.380
A classic machine failure.

39:27.620 --> 39:28.240
Let's play it out.

39:28.860 --> 39:38.000
Let's say your GPS calculates a route for you, and the blue line on the screen tells you to turn right, drive down a ramp, and merge directly into a deep lake.

39:38.000 --> 39:41.200
The map has produced a profound mismatch with reality.

39:41.340 --> 39:42.060
How do you handle it?

39:42.200 --> 39:42.340
Right.

39:42.540 --> 39:48.760
So if you apply the first option, zero-weighting, you look at the route into the lake, and you say, I don't trust that route right now.

39:49.060 --> 39:50.600
So you tap recalculate.

39:51.060 --> 39:56.920
But because you haven't changed the underlying map, the GPS still thinks the lake is a valid geographic destination.

39:57.320 --> 40:05.340
It just zero-weights that specific ramp and calculates the second fastest route, which probably just drives you into the other side of the exact same lake.

40:05.340 --> 40:09.100
Because the lake itself is still considered a valid road by the system's logic.

40:09.300 --> 40:12.840
You only change the weight of the path, not the reality of the destination.

40:13.260 --> 40:13.760
That's exactly.

40:14.360 --> 40:16.780
Okay, so what if you use the second option, refusal?

40:17.420 --> 40:22.420
You drive to the edge of the lake, you see the water, and you put up a physical barricade that says road closed.

40:23.100 --> 40:24.660
You have documented the failure.

40:25.080 --> 40:27.300
The history remembers that this path doesn't work.

40:28.060 --> 40:32.720
But the GPS database still fundamentally categorizes that space as a road.

40:32.840 --> 40:34.220
You've just placed a block on it.

40:34.220 --> 40:34.520
Right.

40:34.640 --> 40:40.180
But what you actually need to do to fix the broken reality is the third option, domain revision.

40:40.680 --> 40:56.440
You need to contact the software engineers who built the GPS, tell them this geographic coordinate is a body of water, not asphalt, and force them to redraw the fundamental boundaries of the map so that the lake is completely subtracted from the domain of drivable surfaces.

40:56.440 --> 40:58.980
That is a flawless translation of the formal types.

40:59.320 --> 41:00.800
Zero weighting changes no domains.

41:01.000 --> 41:02.160
It just shifts attention.

41:02.480 --> 41:06.000
Refusal changes a status within an append-only historical ledger.

41:06.380 --> 41:09.800
But domain revision literally subtracts the option from reality itself.

41:09.800 --> 41:14.660
And Flyxion insists in the monograph that you absolutely cannot treat these as the same thing.

41:14.660 --> 41:27.920
If a curriculum teaches a child to use one single generic X mark for all three of those scenarios, they are actively, which is an epistemic mistake we see full-grown adults make in the real world every single day.

41:27.920 --> 41:43.840
We constantly mistake our personal refusal of an idea with the actual non-existence of that idea, or, on a systemic level, we try to zero weight a broken policy by just ignoring it, when what we actually need to do is a domain revision and rewrite the rules of the system entirely.

41:44.880 --> 41:48.320
Sproul forces a 10-year-old to mathematically understand the difference.

41:48.320 --> 41:55.840
It is an unbelievable tool for critical thinking in a world that is currently overflowing with bad information and broken maps.

41:56.200 --> 42:04.160
But what I find incredibly compelling, perhaps more than the curriculum itself, is that this rigid, brilliant structure wasn't born perfectly formed.

42:04.560 --> 42:07.700
The creators actually made massive mistakes while they were writing it.

42:07.840 --> 42:08.120
Yes.

42:08.680 --> 42:11.660
This brings us to Part V of the monograph, which is my favorite section.

42:11.900 --> 42:15.140
It's titled The Correction History as Monotonic Continuation.

42:15.360 --> 42:18.100
The authors document their own drafting errors.

42:18.100 --> 42:22.660
They literally use their own curriculum's epistemic philosophy to grade their own homework.

42:23.120 --> 42:25.700
It is a beautiful metal layer to the research.

42:26.300 --> 42:34.800
They transparently outline three specific episodes of failure during the drafting process and analyze how they fix them using their own operators.

42:35.120 --> 42:36.400
Let's look at episode one.

42:36.940 --> 42:41.660
They call it the vocabulary collision, and this was a structural disaster for them early on.

42:41.660 --> 42:47.440
It was a fundamental error in how they define their absolute most basic primitive, the pop.

42:47.440 --> 42:53.760
In their early working drafts, they defined a pop as exposing a distinction without evaluating it.

42:54.020 --> 42:57.840
They thought they were keeping the concept gentle and simple for the kindergartners.

42:57.840 --> 43:06.020
But when they took that draft and checked it against the canonical mathematical sphere pop specification, they realized they had completely botched the math.

43:06.640 --> 43:09.280
The official, formal definition of a pop is commitment.

43:10.000 --> 43:14.800
The act of choosing a specific option and permanently removing the others from what remains possible.

43:15.220 --> 43:19.500
Exposing a distinction and committing to a choice are two entirely different mathematical operations.

43:19.500 --> 43:21.300
So how did they resolve the mismatch?

43:21.380 --> 43:23.360
Did they just tweak the wording in the margins?

43:23.820 --> 43:24.140
No.

43:24.680 --> 43:32.940
They had already spent weeks writing and illustrating a complete 16-page draft of Sproul 01 based on that incorrect definition.

43:33.200 --> 43:35.500
And they had to throw the entire thing in the garbage.

43:36.060 --> 43:39.400
They realized that their error wasn't a case of monotonic learning.

43:39.900 --> 43:42.940
They weren't just refining a partial truth into a better truth.

43:43.180 --> 43:46.920
It was a constitutive revision of a completely false premise.

43:46.920 --> 43:51.180
In the vocabulary of Cycle 6 we just learned, Episode 1 was an A-.

43:51.700 --> 43:56.820
It was a domain revision, the map was broken, and they had to scrap it and redraw the boundaries.

43:57.520 --> 44:06.480
This failure actually led directly to the creation of Sproul 00, which now handles the exposing a distinction concept without ever using the word pop,

44:06.880 --> 44:10.820
allowing a completely rewritten Sproul 01 to focus purely on the act of commitment.

44:11.040 --> 44:13.480
It is fascinating to watch them apply their own medicine.

44:13.720 --> 44:13.920
Right.

44:13.920 --> 44:16.640
But then we get to Episode 2, which they call Three Refinements.

44:16.920 --> 44:20.100
This is an example of where monotonic learning actually works properly.

44:20.240 --> 44:23.060
Give us an example of how they refined the text without breaking the map.

44:23.280 --> 44:26.920
Originally, the opening line of Sproul 01 was written as,

44:27.480 --> 44:28.540
Three things could happen.

44:29.280 --> 44:33.640
But upon review, they realized that phrasing was formally imprecise.

44:34.260 --> 44:37.160
Unchosen shapes sitting on a page are not events yet.

44:37.700 --> 44:39.160
Mathematically, they are continuations.

44:39.160 --> 44:40.980
So they changed the text to read.

44:41.580 --> 44:42.980
There are three ways to go.

44:43.360 --> 44:45.620
And that is a perfect example of monotonic learning.

44:45.880 --> 44:47.860
They didn't have to erase what was true.

44:48.140 --> 44:50.280
It was still true that there were three options on the page.

44:50.340 --> 44:53.540
They just narrowed the precision of the language to better match the math.

44:53.800 --> 44:55.220
They didn't have to throw the booklet away.

44:55.480 --> 44:56.780
They just sharpened the focus.

44:56.780 --> 44:59.040
And finally, we have Episode 3, titled,

44:59.560 --> 45:02.060
Gaps Found by Checking, Not by Assumption.

45:03.060 --> 45:05.940
This is where the concept of embodiment proved its worth.

45:06.600 --> 45:11.520
When the authors forced themselves to mathematically prove every single page of the children's books

45:11.520 --> 45:13.740
against the formal formulas in the monograph,

45:14.060 --> 45:16.580
they found massive holes in their own theory.

45:16.740 --> 45:16.880
Hmm.

45:16.880 --> 45:17.280
Yeah.

45:17.420 --> 45:21.340
For example, in Sproul 01, they drew an illustration showing two different histories

45:21.340 --> 45:23.240
branching off from the same starting shapes.

45:23.740 --> 45:26.820
But when they looked at the formal math in Chapter 4 of the monograph,

45:27.860 --> 45:30.360
the math didn't actually have a rule for branching yet.

45:30.880 --> 45:34.980
The formulas only supported a single, linear sequence of events.

45:35.540 --> 45:40.740
So the visual of the children's book was writing checks that the formal mathematical theory couldn't cash.

45:41.180 --> 45:43.140
The illustration was ahead of the physics.

45:43.240 --> 45:43.680
Exactly.

45:43.680 --> 45:46.480
Because the curriculum was strictly embodied,

45:47.100 --> 45:48.820
they weren't allowed to just wave their hands and say,

45:48.940 --> 45:50.920
oh, the kids will understand what the picture means.

45:51.840 --> 45:57.860
The gaps in their academic theory literally revealed themselves as physical impossibilities on the page.

45:58.680 --> 46:02.740
The authors were forced to go back to the drawing board and invent a formal branch rule

46:02.740 --> 46:06.940
and a formal replay definition just so the underlying math would catch up

46:06.940 --> 46:08.920
to what the children's books were demanding to be true.

46:09.220 --> 46:10.900
Flyxion makes a vital point here.

46:10.900 --> 46:14.120
An illustrated curriculum would never have caught this error.

46:14.540 --> 46:17.600
If you are just drawing pretty pictures to go along with a theory,

46:18.100 --> 46:21.400
you never actually test the load-bearing capacity of the theory itself.

46:21.800 --> 46:23.980
You just assume the drywall will hold up the roof.

46:24.400 --> 46:29.500
But because every single page of Sproul had to perfectly map to a formal mathematical definition,

46:30.080 --> 46:33.020
the embodiment process forced the errors to the surface.

46:33.440 --> 46:35.780
The curriculum corrected its own creators.

46:35.780 --> 46:37.900
It is the ultimate proof of concept.

46:38.280 --> 46:43.280
The curriculum works because the agonizing act of writing it proved the curriculum's own thesis.

46:43.780 --> 46:46.520
We have journeyed a remarkably long way today.

46:46.680 --> 46:47.520
We really have.

46:47.900 --> 46:53.420
We started by looking at what seemed like a simple, colorful mobile game of popping parentheses on a screen.

46:53.820 --> 46:57.880
We dug underneath that to find the foundational primitives of all digital logic.

46:58.240 --> 47:00.620
Pop, refuse, bind, and collapse.

47:00.620 --> 47:05.620
We explored how a curriculum uses those primitives to force children to track the history of their choices,

47:06.140 --> 47:09.600
proving that counting is about measuring events, not axiomatic objects.

47:09.900 --> 47:14.380
We saw how that historical foundation scales up into building domains,

47:15.000 --> 47:18.000
mapping arithmetic as a journey rather than a static pile,

47:18.440 --> 47:22.780
and grappling with the profound reality of information loss in forgetful machines.

47:22.780 --> 47:28.680
And finally, we climbed all the way up into an epistemic framework for fixing broken realities,

47:29.240 --> 47:33.240
learning the crucial, life-altering difference between zero-weighting a bad option,

47:33.640 --> 47:36.820
refusing an idea, and erasing a broken map entirely.

47:37.600 --> 47:42.960
You, the listener, now have a formidable shortcut to being well-informed about the architecture of knowledge itself.

47:43.500 --> 47:47.840
The next time you face an intractable problem at work or a misunderstanding in a relationship,

47:47.840 --> 47:50.880
or you're just trying to decipher the bias in a news article,

47:51.380 --> 47:54.200
ask yourself, am I looking at the full history of choices,

47:54.480 --> 47:56.700
or am I just looking at the collapsed final picture?

47:56.900 --> 47:59.900
Because as Sproul02 so elegantly teaches us,

47:59.960 --> 48:02.360
the final picture doesn't always tell you what happened first.

48:02.680 --> 48:03.840
Only the history remembers.

48:04.220 --> 48:08.920
Which leaves us with one final, lingering thought for you to mull over as you go about your day.

48:09.980 --> 48:13.520
If a final picture cannot tell you the history that created it,

48:13.520 --> 48:19.620
and if we, as humans, interact almost exclusively with final pictures in our daily lives,

48:20.120 --> 48:22.580
you know, we read the finished news headlines, we buy the finished products,

48:22.760 --> 48:25.420
we judge the finalized personalities of the people we meet,

48:25.420 --> 48:30.860
how many completely different, incredibly complex histories are we blindly treating as identical?

48:31.360 --> 48:34.480
And what happens to our reality when we assume the wrong one?

48:34.940 --> 48:37.020
That is the real danger of trusting the X-ray.

48:37.180 --> 48:39.820
Until next time, keep digging beneath the picture.

